{
  "image_name": "1c1f8cf76d9f66220fcbf94b4e1207da.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes the arachidonic acid and phospholipid pathways linked to vascular inflammation, showing where several anti-inflammatory cardiovascular drugs act. Inside the cell, arachidonic acid is converted into two major mediator groups: leukotrienes through 5-lipoxygenase/FLAP, and prostaglandins or thromboxane through COX-1 and COX-2. These products then signal outside the cell through specific receptors, including LTB4 receptors, cysteinyl-leukotriene receptors, and the thromboxane receptor.\n\nThe main message is a comparison of therapeutic targets across this network. Atreleuton blocks 5-LO and veliflapon blocks FLAP, reducing leukotriene formation. NSAIDs and coxibs inhibit COX enzymes, limiting prostaglandin and thromboxane production. Downstream receptor antagonists are also shown: montelukast at cysteinyl-leukotriene receptors and terutroban at the thromboxane receptor.\n\nA parallel pathway involves phospholipases acting on lipoproteins and membrane phospholipids. sPLA2 contributes to LDL modification and is inhibited by varespladib. Lp-PLA2 acts on oxidized phospholipids to generate lysophosphatidylcholine, and this step is inhibited by darapladib. Overall, the diagram highlights multiple intervention points aimed at dampening lipid-derived inflammatory signaling in cardiovascular disease.",
